  "article_id": "gnews:3345f0d91321165a099860c9",
  "title": "Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe｜Daily News Digest｜2026-07-02｜web only",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe\nJuly 2, 2026 -- Today’s top stories: Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe, Rubio Would Step Aside for Vance in 2028 White House Race, and Google Ordered To Pay Klarna $1.97b in Antitrust Damages.\nBy CommonWealth Magazine\nSingapore seizes $42m home in Nvidia chip smuggling case\nSingapore authorities have seized a multimillion-dollar mansion and charged people with money laundering as part of a case of suspected c",
  "passage": "Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe\nJuly 2, 2026 -- Today’s top stories: Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe, Rubio Would Step Aside for Vance in 2028 White House Race, and Google Ordered To Pay Klarna $1.97b in Antitrust Damages.\nBy CommonWealth Magazine\nSingapore seizes $42m home in Nvidia chip smuggling case\nSingapore authorities have seized a multimillion-dollar mansion and charged people with money laundering as part of a case of suspected cross-border smuggling of hardware containing Nvidia chips.\nA prohibition of disposal order was issued against the luxury home, valued at about SGD 55M ($42M), freezing the asset and preventing its purchase or sale. Funds in bank accounts under investigation, valued at about SGD 1M, were also seized.\nLim Jenny and Woon Guo Jie Aaron, two people suspected of involvement in the alleged smuggling, face money laundering charges. Four firms allegedly linked to the export of computer servers that might contain Nvidia chips to Malaysia are facing charges for false representation.\nThe companies are Aperia International, A-Speed Infotech, and Aperia Cloud Services (II) — collectively known as the Aperia group — and Luxuriate Your Life.\nThis comes after a US…",
  "statement": "Singapore authorities have seized a multimillion-dollar mansion and charged people with money laundering as part of a case of suspected cross-border smuggling of hardware containing Nvidia chips"
 },
 {
  "id": "G535",
  "article_id": "gnews:dbff79c9778e4b3d8ac8eef2",
  "title": "Nokia advances AI-RAN toward commercial deployment and physical AI, launches sovereign agentic AI platform for telco autonomy with NVIDIA",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-21",
  "article_opening": "Nokia advances AI-RAN toward commercial deployment and physical AI, launches sovereign agentic AI platform for telco autonomy with NVIDIA\nNew capabilities improve radio performance, enable real-time intelligence at the edge and extend governed AI agents across network operations.\nNokia AI-native anyRAN software and NVIDIA accelerated computing turn the radio access network into a distributed platform for sensing, intelligence and real-time action.\nBT, Deutsche Telekom, Elisa and Orange provide g",
  "passage": "…ses existing radio infrastructure to identify RF threats, interference and rogue transmitters.\nZaiNar, a Physical AI company building real-time spatial intelligence technology, is integrating its positioning software with Nokia Cloud AI-RAN to enable precise, network-based location capabilities using existing wireless infrastructure, without requiring changes to connected devices or radio hardware.\nAt NVIDIA GTC Berlin, Nokia is demonstrating integrated sensing and communications, showing how the RAN can detect and understand activity in the physical environment. Nokia and NVIDIA are working with a growing infrastructure ecosystem to provide the deployment foundation for these capabilities:\nDell Technologies, Quanta Cloud Technology and Supermicro are supporting flexible NVIDIA Aerial RAN Computer configurations for distributed and centralized deployments that run Nokia 5G anyRAN software alongside AI inference workloads.\nRed Hat is bringing AI-RAN technologies onto a common cloud-native platform with Red Hat OpenShift, creating a secure foundation for RAN functions and AI workloads on shared infrastructure.\nSovereign agentic AI platform for telco autonomy, co-created by Nokia and NVIDIA\nNokia and NVIDIA are also advancing their collaboration on agentic AI for trusted, sovereign, cross-domain network autonomy. NVIDIA AI Enterprise software and NVIDIA Nemotron models and libraries are being integrated into Nokia’s agentic platform, allowing operators to build, evaluate, deploy and operate telco-spec…",
  "statement": "Dell Technologies, Quanta Cloud Technology and Supermicro are supporting flexible NVIDIA Aerial RAN Computer configurations"
 },
 {
  "id": "G130",
  "article_id": "gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1",
  "title": "Here's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats Itself",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "The artificial intelligence chip giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) has struggled recently and is now down nearly 13% over the past month (as of July 1). After years of an incredible AI rally, doubts are now creeping into investors' minds.\nThere are questions about whether the large tech companies can continue to invest hundreds of billions annually in AI infrastructure, which is driving much of the cycle, and whether constraints such as energy and power will eventually have a real impact. For Nvidia ",
  "passage": "…ould challenge the operating system Nvidia has built for developers using its graphics processing units (GPUs) to train AI models, called Compute Unified Device Architecture (CUDA) .\nNvidia launched CUDA in 2006 and has built a whole ecosystem around it, so this is easier said than done, but it nonetheless represents a substantial part of Nvidia's moat and therefore would be devastating to the company if CUDA loses its grip on the market.\nAs you can see in the chart above, Nvidia has had an average forward price-to-earnings ratio of nearly 34 for the past two years. Wall Street consensus estimates suggest the company will earn adjusted earnings per share of $8.97 in its current fiscal year.\nApplying a 34 forward P/E, the stock would be worth roughly $305 per share, implying about 54% upside from current levels. This means a $25,000 investment in Nvidia would be worth over $38,400 if history repeats itself.\nNow, investors should understand that there's no guarantee this plays out. Nvidia, at its current market cap, is up against the law of large numbers. As companies get more mature, they typically face lower multiples. Competition could also be a real concern.\nHowever, Nvidia has traded at a 34 forward P/E before, so it could do so again should the company prove investors wrong about their concerns.\nHere's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats Itself\nBy Bram Berkowitz – Jul 3, 2026 at 12:05PM EST",
  "statement": "Applying a 34 forward P/E, the stock would be worth roughly $305 per share, implying about 54% upside from current levels"
 },
 {
  "id": "G474",
  "article_id": "gnews:621e1b678a037049c86a70af",
  "title": "Nvidia vs. Micron: Which Is the Better AI Semiconductor Stock to Own for the Next 5 Years?",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-04",
  "article_opening": "Artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure stocks are still one of the biggest driving forces in the market. While Nvidia ( NVDA +2.53% ) helped lead the early charge, other stocks, like Micron ( MU +4.20% ) , have greatly outperformed the AI leader over the past year.\nLet's dive into both AI stocks to see which one looks set to outperform over the next five years.\nNvidia was the first big AI winner, and the advantages the company established remain in place today. Meanwhile, despite its massiv",
  "passage": "Artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure stocks are still one of the biggest driving forces in the market. While Nvidia ( NVDA +2.53% ) helped lead the early charge, other stocks, like Micron ( MU +4.20% ) , have greatly outperformed the AI leader over the past year.\nLet's dive into both AI stocks to see which one looks set to outperform over the next five years.\nNvidia was the first big AI winner, and the advantages the company established remain in place today. Meanwhile, despite its massive size, the company continues to put up incredible growth. In Q2 2027, it more than doubled its revenue to a whopping $96.2 billion, with 117% growth in its data center segment. Meanwhile, it projected growth of 89% next quarter and 70% for fiscal 2028 (ending January 2028), while saying it remains capacity-constrained. In other words, it would be growing even faster if it could make more chips.\nAt the heart of Nvidia's growth story remains its graphics processing units (GPUs) and its CUDA software platform. Most early AI code was written using CUDA and optimized for its GPUs, which has given the company a wide moat in AI model training. That moat remains intact today, but the company is much more than just GPUs.\nToday, Nvidia is a complete AI infrastructure company, offering full server racks designed for specific AI tasks, such as training, inference , and agentic AI. The company took a big leap in the inference market following its \"acquisition\" of Groq and…",
  "statement": "it projected growth of 89% next quarter and 70% for fiscal 2028 (ending January 2028)"
 },
 {
  "id": "G133",
  "article_id": "gnews:8c9d215416b714064de3b095",
  "title": "NVIDIA Quietly Makes Omniverse Free for Production Use",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA has dropped the subscription requirement for Omniverse . The platform is now free for development, production, and redistribution, with no NVIDIA AI Enterprise subscription required. For a product that carried a $4,500-per-GPU-per-year list price under NVIDIA AI Enterprise, and whose Nucleus server pricing prompted a developer forum thread titled “Pricing: $25,000 for the nucleus server!?”, this is one of the larger licensing resets NVIDIA has made. It’s great news for the community, but ",
  "passage": "…l AI: the world models that let robots and vehicles reason about environments they have never seen.\nA great example is Alpamayo, the family of open autonomous vehicle models NVIDIA introduced at CES 2026. Alpamayo 1 is a 10-billion-parameter vision-language-action model, built on Cosmos, that generates driving trajectories along with chain-of-causation reasoning traces explaining why it acted. Models like that cannot be trained or validated on road miles alone; rare and dangerous edge cases are better off simulated than on busy streets. That is what the AlpaSim blueprint and NVIDIA’s neural reconstruction pipelines do, and the Omniverse with OpenUSD is the underlying simulation environment. The first production deployment, the Mercedes-Benz CLA on the DRIVE platform, reached the US market this year. Every developer who wants to work in that stack now gets the simulation layer without a license conversation.\nDigital Twins, Up to and Including the AI Factory\nA digital twin in this context is not a 3D model; it is a physically accurate, continuously updated simulation of a real facility, built from SimReady assets that carry mass, friction, and material properties, described in OpenUSD so that data from CAD, PLM, and simulation tools stays interoperable. The point is to run the facility in software before and during operation in the physical world: test a robot fleet’s routing on a virtual factory floor, validate camera placement, rehearse a production line change, and only then commit st…",
  "statement": "The first production deployment, the Mercedes-Benz CLA on the DRIVE platform, reached the US market this year."
 },
 {
  "id": "G528",
  "article_id": "gnews:bc86d298ecece0269dc9e856",
  "title": "'This computer is going to be incredible': Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang explains why the new Surface Laptop Ultra is \"really a work of love\"",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-17",
  "article_opening": "'This computer is going to be incredible': Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang explains why the new Surface Laptop Ultra is \"really a work of love\"\nBy Mike Moore\nNvidia CEO sings the praises of the new Surface Laptop Ultra\nNvidia CEO Jensen Huang has sung the praises of Microsoft's new Surface Laptop Ultra , describing it as \"so beautiful\" and \"incredible\".\nSpeaking at a developer event in London, Huang outlined the development process that went into building the hardware powering the new product, which is ",
  "passage": "'This computer is going to be incredible': Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang explains why the new Surface Laptop Ultra is \"really a work of love\"\nBy Mike Moore\nNvidia CEO sings the praises of the new Surface Laptop Ultra\nNvidia CEO Jensen Huang has sung the praises of Microsoft's new Surface Laptop Ultra , describing it as \"so beautiful\" and \"incredible\".\nSpeaking at a developer event in London, Huang outlined the development process that went into building the hardware powering the new product, which is the first Microsoft device powered by an Nvidia chip.\n“This computer is going to be incredible - it took us four years to build it,\" Huang told attendees including TechRadar Pro , \"this project is really a work of love - we had several thousand engineering years dedicated to it, we build a brand new revolutionary chip, a whole new line of PCs, it’s so beautiful - you’re going to love it.”\nThe end of the traditional PC?\nRevealed in September 2026, the Surface Laptop Ultra is targeted towards developer and AI usage, powered by Nvidia’s RTX Spark platform with an N1X processor and RTX Blackwell graphics, supporting up to 128GB unified memory\nThis is alongside 24GB RAM and 512GB storage, while the next version has 32GB RAM and 1TB storage, making…",
  "statement": "the new product, which is the first Microsoft device powered by an Nvidia chip"
 },
 {
  "id": "G216",
  "article_id": "gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Below $200 Per Share Again. Here's When It Could Hit $300.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "For a long time, Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) was priced above $200 per share. Now, it has fallen below that after a few days of heavy selling pressure. The stock is down around 16% from its highs and is up a mere 6% for the year. That's a pretty disappointing result for most investors since Nvidia has been such a strong stock pick over the past few years.\nHowever, I still think the stock still has a bright future. In fact, I think it could easily rise over 50% to reach $300. But when might that occur",
  "passage": "…slowing down anytime soon.\nDuring its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\nThat sounds like further revenue growth ahead, and that's exactly what Wall Street analysts are projecting as well. For the rest of this year, they expect 82% growth. For 2027, that figure rises to 41%. Those are strong growth rates and will likely lead to major profits as well.\nFor fiscal 2028, ending January 2028, the average analyst projects $12.76 in earnings per share. I think that's a conservative estimate, because the analyst community has consistently underprojected Nvidia's growth.\nIf we use that figure and value the stock at a reasonable earnings multiple of 25, the projected share price at the end of fiscal 2028 is $319. As a result, I think the stock could easily reach $300 per share sometime in late 2027.\nThat's 50% upside in about a year and a half, which is a great return in a short time frame . Furthermore, most of this growth is already known because management likely has good information on customer orders over the next year. So I think Nvidia is an excellent buy on this most recent round of sell-offs.\nBy Keithen Drury – Jul 6, 2026 at 8:53AM EST",
  "statement": "If we use that figure and value the stock at a reasonable earnings multiple of 25, the projected share price at the end of fiscal 2028 is $319."
 },
 {
  "id": "G176",
  "article_id": "gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587",
  "title": "Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-05",
  "article_opening": "Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks rais",
  "passage": "…es need to train and run their most advanced models.\nThe design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\nThe setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\n\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\nNVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\nThe reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits.\nA backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\" SemiAnalysis said.\nThat leaves Nvidia with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\" SemiAnalysis said, predicti…",
  "statement": "NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes"
 },
 {
  "id": "G198",
  "article_id": "gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd",
  "title": "Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double the Last One, and Memory Is Why. What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For.\nNvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%. This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams ",
  "passage": "…ud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\nThe memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\nAs a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\nOnly a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\nFor enterprise technology buyers, that means memory has become a strategic budget variable rather than a rounding error, whether the purchase is a data center commitment or a fleet refresh.\nThree considerations fol…",
  "statement": "As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent."
 },
 {
  "id": "G160",
  "article_id": "gnews:ff7d99387d1dae92ede72145",
  "title": "Sentiment Could Be Turning Sour on Nvidia. Here’s Where 1 Analyst Thinks NVDA Stock Is Headed Next.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-05",
  "article_opening": "Perhaps the most important stock in the market, Nvidia (NVDA) remains a top stock I continue to watch closely.\nThere are a few reasons for this. Of course, as the world's largest semiconductor company, the fact that Nvidia powers the world's economy (essentially) via propping up the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution is a massive deal. Indeed, the range of opinions on where NVDA stock could be headed from here varies greatly, and we'll get to that shortly.\nDear Netflix Stock Fans, Mark Your",
  "passage": "…cular financing in any time or sector isn't generally a positive when we look at the overall health of any major trend.\nFrom here, I think data center construction, and the ability for Nvidia's clients (many of the largest tech companies) to continue to support massive construction plans, could be what investors will be watching more closely than Nvidia's earnings reports. That's what I think we're seeing in this recent selloff in NVDA stock, which is now down around 17% from its recent all-time high.\nThe good news for investors in Nvidia who are willing to look past these concerns is that most analysts on Wall Street tend to have a similarly bullish stance on the world's leading chipmaker.\nThe consensus price target for NVDA stock sits a little higher than $300 per share, implying more than 50% upside from here. Even more notable is the high target on the street of $500 per share, which suggests Nvidia could more than double over the course of the next year, if all goes well.\nI'm not so sure that those bullish expectations will come to bear, at least not this year. Sentiment has weakened considerably, given the inflationary pressures on consumers and growing concerns around the business models of the companies that support the artificial intelligence revolution.\nFor now, I'm not willing to put my own \"sell\" rating on Nvidia, but I'm cautious. To me, NVDA stock looks more like a hold right now than anything else.",
  "statement": "The consensus price target for NVDA stock sits a little higher than $300 per share"
 },
 {
  "id": "G013",
  "article_id": "gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a",
  "title": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reach",
  "passage": "…nd highlighted that the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1, meaning the ratio of CPUs needed to support the GPU is actually increasing, not decreasing (the prior narrative was that GPUs would ultimately take over the CPU role).\nJensen Huang’s decision to build the DGX Rubin NVL8 on Xeon 6 coupled with their $5B equity investment in Intel represents the strongest signal yet from a third party that Intel’s CPU is competitive. This is not an Apple rumor or an announcement on social media: This is a CPU architecture decision from the world’s largest infrastructure company in AI.\nQ1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 while GAAP was negative $0.73 (this was after heavy foundry investment charges). Meanwhile, Intel Foundry saw revenue of $5.4B (up 16% year-over-year), which translates to a Foundry segment that is now over $20B in annualised revenue (still negative earnings but not quite the rounding error it was). CFO David Zinsner characterized the quarter as having “unprecedented demand for silicon” and Q2 guidance is $13.8 to $14.8B, indicating continued sequential revenue strength. About 60% of this quarter's revenue mix now comes from AI-linked sources, compared with a little less than 25% a year ago.\nThe Google Deal — Multiyear, Locked Pricing, Custom ASIC Co-Develop…",
  "statement": "Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year)"
 },
 {
  "id": "G059",
  "article_id": "gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816",
  "title": "Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut\nBy Luke James\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs.\nNvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co",
  "passage": "…in exchange for a slice of their future sales, but neither Nvidia nor its partners has disclosed the revenue-split percentages.\nIt’s no secret that the credit-support side of this model will help to address a financing gap that Nvidia itself has identified. Even signed, long-term customer commitments have failed to convince lenders to fund large-scale deployments, leaving smaller clouds unable to borrow against the demand they had already generated.\nIn an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions. Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\nNvidia has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing , arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism. The new model inverts that: rather than investing capital that returns as GPU orders, Nvidia extends credit support and collects a royalty on its partners’ sales for years afterward.\nThat royalty also ties a sl…",
  "statement": "The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results"
 },
 {
  "id": "G243",
  "article_id": "gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a",
  "title": "NVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "/ NVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?\nNVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?\nNVIDIA Corporation NVDA shares have declined 6.6% over the past month, underperforming the broader Zacks Computer and Technology sector, which slipped 2.2% during the same period. At first glance, the decline may look worrying, but a closer look suggests the weakness is more about market sentiment than company-specific problems.\nThe sell-off has been wides",
  "passage": "…orms like CUDA and its growing portfolio of AI software make it difficult for customers to switch to competing solutions. This creates a powerful competitive moat that extends well beyond hardware.\nAs AI adoption shifts from model training toward large-scale inference and enterprise deployment, NVIDIA appears well-positioned to benefit across multiple stages of the AI investment cycle.\nNVIDIA’s Financial Performance Remains Exceptional\nNVIDIA's financial performance continues to justify investor confidence. First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion, while non-GAAP earnings per share jumped 140% to $1.87.\nManagement also expects the momentum to continue. For the second quarter of fiscal 2027, NVIDIA projects revenues of approximately $91 billion, representing nearly 95% year-over-year growth. The company also expects a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%, up from 72.7% a year ago, reflecting healthy pricing power and operational efficiency.\nAnalysts remain optimistic as well. The Zacks Consensus Estimate points to continued revenue and earnings growth in both fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, suggesting that demand for NVIDIA's AI products remains far from slowing.\nThe company's cash generation is equally impressive. During the first quarter, NVIDIA generated $50.3 billion in operating cash flow and $48.6 billion in free cash flow. It ended the quarter with $80.6 billion in cash, cash equivalents and marketable securities, up from $62.6 billion in the previous quarter.\nThis enormous financial flexibility allows NVIDIA to invest aggressively in r…",
  "statement": "For the second quarter of fiscal 2027, NVIDIA projects revenues of approximately $91 billion, representing nearly 95% year-over-year growth. The company also expects a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%"
 },
 {
  "id": "G255",
  "article_id": "gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32",
  "title": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\nBy Danielle Liverance\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewa",
  "passage": "…0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about locking in its own memory supply as other companies like Apple (Nasdaq: AAPL) have been caught flat footed. And yet, the combined value of Micron, SK Hynix, and Samsung now rivals NVIDIA itself.\nMost strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. Simply put, the rise of memory is shifting the center of gravity in the data center trade away from NVIDIA and towards memory names.\nWhich brings the market to the quiet question underneath all of it. Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now havi…",
  "statement": "Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability."
 },
 {
  "id": "G165",
  "article_id": "gnews:d1a7a7f18dce5c8ae5e4d0ae",
  "title": "Singapore files new charges in Nvidia chip fraud case",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-05",
  "article_opening": "Singapore files new charges in Nvidia chip fraud case\nSingaporean prosecutors filed additional charges including money laundering against a key suspect in an AI server fraud case.\n(July 6): Singaporean prosecutors filed additional charges including money laundering against a key suspect in an artificial intelligence (AI) server fraud case, part of the Southeast Asian nation's growing efforts to clamp down on Nvidia Corp chips being diverted illicitly to other locations including China.\nAlan Wei ",
  "passage": "…ontrols. They have also pressed charges of fraud by false representation against Wei.\nThe high-profile case centres on whether executives including Wei at Aperia Group misled Dell Technologies Inc, Super Micro Computer Inc and Asustek Computer Inc by misrepresenting the actual end-users of the hardware they acquired from these server suppliers. It also sheds light on how advanced US chip technology subject to trade curbs is channelled through Southeast Asia, and potentially finding its way to China.\nProsecutors have not accused Nvidia, Dell, Super Micro or Asustek of any wrongdoing, but instead are focusing on the individual actors at Aperia and another Chinese citizen involved in the case. Senior government officials have repeatedly said they will clamp down on attempts to use Singapore-linked firms to evade foreign trade restrictions.\nBetween November 2023 and February 2025, three Aperia executives - Wei, Aaron Woon Guo Jie and Jenny Lim - falsely told the suppliers that one of the Aperia Group's companies would be the end-user of the servers, according to a police statement published on July 1.\nBoth Woon and Lim have also been charged with fraud. The two executives have not indicated how they are going to plead their case.\nAperia said companies in the group and Wei are \"fully cooperating with the authorities\" in a statement on Monday. \"It is unfortunate that despite their best efforts, the companies face these allegations of wrongdoing, which they deny,\" Aperia added.\nOne of the money laundering charges against Wei conc…",
  "statement": "Senior government officials have repeatedly said they will clamp down on attempts to use Singapore-linked firms to evade foreign trade restrictions."
 },
 {
  "id": "G233",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "…uilt datacenter. This project was financed using a $10B USD facility led by Blackstone (Tactical Opportunities, Credit and Insurance) and supported by Coatue.\nThe Nvidia backstop allows Firmus to grow their business by an order of magnitude. As per Nvidia’s key objectives that we outlined above, the focus of this cluster will be to open up compute to a variety of AI Natives, Enterprises and Inference Providers, with many different rental tenors to be offered. Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\nBeyond this – Firmus also announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will also underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032. Firmus will still need to identify a datacenter provider or build their own datacenters before they can deploy GPUs.\nWe have seen many other neoclouds having backstops, but many are not public yet.\nNvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if t…",
  "statement": "Firmus also announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will also underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032"
 },
 {
  "id": "G151",
  "article_id": "gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4",
  "title": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing\nNvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure. This initiative is designed to support startups and enterprises in scaling AI operations.\nNvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.\nThe model aims to facilitate access to large-scale AI compute for startups and enterprises.\nSharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of th",
  "passage": "…upply, even when demand is evident. Nvidia's strategy aims to alleviate this bottleneck by aligning its financial interests more closely with cloud providers serving these customers.\nAs the AI landscape evolves, suppliers like Nvidia are adapting to the changing economics of AI services. With more companies transitioning from experimental phases to commercial products, there is a growing need for computing supply that can scale with usage while maintaining flexibility in payment structures during the shift from pilot projects to full deployment.\nNvidia highlighted the needs of model builders, inference providers, and enterprises requiring immediate cloud access for various AI-related tasks. The company also pointed to AI-native businesses such as Baseten, Fireworks AI, and Together AI as examples of where demand is heading.\nThese entities depend on reliable access to accelerated computing as customer usage increases, often requiring more adaptable commercial arrangements than traditional data center buildouts or fixed infrastructure commitments allow.\nFor Nvidia, this model not only creates a recurring income stream tied to the usage of supported cloud capacity but also deepens its involvement in the AI cloud market as competition intensifies among providers aiming to serve developers, digital-native companies, and established enterprises.\nSharon AI's co-founder and CEO, James Manning, described the agreement as a milestone for the company's plans, stating, \"This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a piv…",
  "statement": "The company also pointed to AI-native businesses such as Baseten, Fireworks AI, and Together AI as examples of where demand is heading."
 },
 {
  "id": "G417",
  "article_id": "gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb",
  "title": "Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-14",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington | Tom's Hardware\nNvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\nBy Jowi Morales\nIt's now harder to buy Nvidia AI ",
  "passage": "…to China. This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip smuggling probe. Nvidia was not immediately available for comment on the news.\nAlthough the U.S. has banned the latest AI GPUs for export into China since 2022, various investigations showed Chinese companies could still easily get their hands on these coveted chips until recently. Washington’s and its allies’ crackdown on AI GPU smuggling have cut supply in China, which is now making it harder for AI companies to procure the processors they need. President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation. However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough. One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\nAs Nvidia reportedly cleaned up its verified list of clients and made it harder for non-vetted companies to acquire its chips, the company has also told its partners to fix their export control compliance. “We insist our partners are compliant,” Nvidia CEO Jense…",
  "statement": "President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region"
 },
 {
  "id": "G140",
  "article_id": "gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128",
  "title": "Nvidia Launches GPU Backstop Financing Model, Takes Cut of Cloud Revenue From Neocloud Partners",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia will act as a financial backstop for neocloud GPU deployments, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of cloud revenue generated on that capacity [1]\nSharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement; Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3]\nThe revenue-share percentage was not disclosed; Nvidia declined to provide details beyond its ",
  "passage": "… investors [1] [3] . In exchange, Nvidia earns both its standard product revenue from the hardware sale and a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity [1] .\nThe model addresses a persistent bottleneck in the neocloud sector: smaller operators often have customer demand in hand but cannot secure financing quickly enough to build infrastructure, because lenders view GPU residual values as uncertain [4] . By backstopping idle capacity, Nvidia effectively underwrites the asset, making GPU clusters more bankable. The company framed the program as accelerating 'adoption of Nvidia platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector' [1] .\nSharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6] . The company raised an additional $1.6 billion in private placement in June 2026 to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory and has partnered with VAST Data for 600PB of storage infrastructure supporting roughly 100,000 GPUs across its sovereign cloud operations in Australia and the Asia-Pacific region [6] .\nFirmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] . Both operators plan to serve AI-native comp…",
  "statement": "Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO"
 },
